==English==

===Adverb===
{{head|en|adverb|head=[[from]] [[time]] [[to]] [[time]]}}

# {{context|idiomatic|lang=en}} [[occasionally|Occasionally]]; [[sometimes]]; [[once in a while]].
#* {{circa|1595}} [[w:William_Shakespeare|William Shakespeare]], ''Romeo and Juliet'', act 3, sc. 3.
#*: I'll find out your man,
#*: And he shall signify '''from time to time'''
#*: Every good hap to you that chances here.
#* '''1815''', [[w:Walter_Scott|Sir Walter Scott]], ''Guy Mannering'', ch. 25,
#*: On these red embers Hatteraick '''from time to time''' threw a handful of twigs.
#* '''1922''', [[w:T._S._Eliot|T. S. Eliot]], "The Waste Land," lines 196-197,
#*: But at my back '''from time to time''' I hear
#*: The sound of horns and motors.
# {{rfquote-sense}} {{context|legal|lang=en}} In whatever status exists at various times.{{attention|en|topic=law|definition may need improved wording}}
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} Continuously from one time to another; at all times, [[constantly]].
#* '''1590''', Edmund Spenser, ''The Faerie Queene'', III.v:
#*: So was she trayned vp '''from time to time''', / In all chast vertue, and true bounti-hed / Till to her dew perfection she was ripened.

